  "answer_text": "There are **three ways** to change the color of a `<span>` on mouse over. Here they are, from simplest to most flexible:\n\n---\n\n## \u2705 Method 1: CSS `:hover` Pseudo-class (Recommended)\n\nThis is the cleanest and most efficient approach. If you can identify an element by ID (`#`) or class (`.`), you can add the `:hover` selector to make changes only appear when the user has moused over an element.\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head>\n    <style>\n      span {\n        color: black;\n      }\n\n      span:hover {\n        color: red;\n      }\n    </style>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <span>Hover over me!</span>\n  </body>\n</html>\n```\n\n> \ud83d\udca1 You can also target a specific span using a class (`.mySpan:hover`) or an ID (`#mySpan:hover`).\n\n---\n\n## \u2705 Method 2: Inline JavaScript with `onmouseover` / `onmouseout`\n\nThe JavaScript code can be attached inside quotes to an HTML tag using event handlers like `onmouseover` and `onmouseout`, which replace the CSS selector `:hover`.\n\n```html\n<span\n  onmouseover=\"this.style.color='red';\"\n  onmouseout=\"this.style.color='black';\"\n>\n  Hover over me!\n</span>\n```\n\n> Inside those quotes, use the JavaScript keyword `this` to refer to whatever HTML element the user is interacting with. Add the property `.style.` onto the element to change its style, and convert hyphenated CSS attributes (like `color`) to camelCase JavaScript ones (like `backgroundColor`).\n\n---\n\n## \u2705 Method 3: External JavaScript with Event Listeners (Most Flexible)\n\nAdding JavaScript inside HTML tags (\"inline\") is inflexible and time-consuming. Fortunately, you can change styles of HTML elements in JavaScript at the top of your page or in a separate file.\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <body>\n    <span id=\"mySpan\">Hover over me!</span>\n\n    <script>\n      const span = document.getElementById(\"mySpan\");\n\n      span.addEventListener(\"mouseover\", function () {\n        this.style.color = \"red\";\n      });\n\n      span.addEventListener(\"mouseout\", function () {\n        this.style.color = \"black\";\n      });\n    </script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n```\n\nThe `mouseover` event is used to change the color of an HTML element. To restore the color after moving the mouse away, the `mouseout` event is also used, which fires when the mouse pointer is removed from the element.\n\n---\n\n## \ud83c\udfc6 Which Method Should You Use?\n\n| Method | Pros | Cons |\n|---|---|---|\n| **CSS `:hover`** | Simple, clean, no JS needed | Limited to CSS-based changes |\n| **Inline JS** | Quick to write | Hard to maintain, mixes HTML & JS |\n| **JS Event Listeners** | Flexible, reusable, clean | Slightly more code |\n\n\ud83d\udc49 **CSS `:hover` is recommended** for simply changing color, as it's the most concise and maintainable solution.",
